# Gmail channel adapter — OAuth 2.0 (installed-app flow)
#
# Why three vars: Google's refresh-token flow requires all three. The agent
# never sees a user-facing consent screen at runtime; the refresh token is
# minted once during setup and kept in the secret store.
#
# ── One-time setup ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1. Open https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials
# 2. Create (or pick) a GCP project. Enable the Gmail API:
#       https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/library/gmail.googleapis.com
# 3. Configure the OAuth consent screen (External or Internal). Add the scope
#       https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.modify
#    (Modify is required because receive() removes the UNREAD label after
#    fetch. If you only need send(), gmail.send is narrower; mix-and-match
#    won't work for this adapter.)
# 4. Create an OAuth 2.0 Client ID, type "Desktop app". Copy the client id
#    and secret into GMAIL_CLIENT_ID / GMAIL_CLIENT_SECRET below.
# 5. Mint the refresh token. Easiest path is the OAuth 2.0 Playground:
#       https://developers.google.com/oauthplayground/
#    a. Click the gear icon → check "Use your own OAuth credentials" → paste
#       the client id + secret.
#    b. In the left panel, scroll to "Gmail API v1" and select
#       https://www.googleapis.com/auth/gmail.modify
#    c. Click "Authorize APIs" → sign in with the Gmail account the agent
#       will speak as → grant consent.
#    d. Click "Exchange authorization code for tokens". Copy the
#       refresh_token value into GMAIL_REFRESH_TOKEN below.
#    Alternative: run a 30-line CLI helper that listens on localhost,
#    redirects to Google's auth screen, and prints the refresh token. Skipped
#    here to keep the adapter self-contained — paste-from-Playground works.
#
# ── Vars ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
GMAIL_CLIENT_ID=
GMAIL_CLIENT_SECRET=
GMAIL_REFRESH_TOKEN=

# ── Operational notes ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# • Default path is PULL: call receive() on a timer (every 30–60s is fine for
#   most inboxes). It returns one message at a time so the agent's runloop
#   stays simple. Mark-read is part of receive(), so no double-processing.
#
# • Push path (Pub/Sub watch) is out of scope for this adapter:
#     https://developers.google.com/gmail/api/guides/push
#   It needs a GCP Pub/Sub topic + push subscription + verified domain for
#   the webhook. If you wire it later, the receive() shape can absorb the
#   PubsubMessage payload — Gmail's push only delivers a historyId, so you
#   still call users.history.list to enumerate new messages.
#
# • Quota: Gmail API is 1,000,000,000 quota units/day per project (~one query
#   = 5 units, send = 100). Polling every 30s = 2880 calls/day per agent —
#   negligible at any reasonable scale.
#
# • Domain-wide delegation (Workspace service accounts) is not supported by
#   this adapter; use the installed-app refresh-token flow above.
